@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+---
+id: dl-001-validate-whole-day-utc-periods-via-millisecond-midnight-modulo
+type: decision-log
+title: "Validate whole-day UTC periods via millisecond-midnight modulo"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Rule 3 requires bookings to cover whole UTC days only: a period must start and end at midnight UTC, with
+`end` being the start of the day after the last rented day. `quote` and `book` both need to reject any
+period that doesn't fit this shape, before computing a duration in days.
+
+## Decision
+
+Parse `start`/`end` with `Date.parse` (rejecting unparseable strings), then require
+`timestamp % 86_400_000 === 0` for both. Unix time has no leap seconds, so every UTC midnight is exactly
+a multiple of one day in milliseconds — this check is both necessary and sufficient to confirm midnight
+UTC, without manual string parsing or a date library. Also require `end > start`, so the shortest
+bookable period is one whole day. The number of days is then `(endMs - startMs) / 86_400_000`, always a
+whole number once the modulo check passes.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- No date-handling dependency was needed, keeping the module dependency-free as required.
+- Both plain date strings (`"2024-06-01"`) and explicit midnight timestamps
+  (`"2024-06-01T00:00:00Z"`) are accepted, since both parse to the same UTC midnight instant.
+- Any non-midnight time (e.g. partial days, timezone-offset strings that don't land on UTC midnight) is
+  rejected by construction.
