@@ -0,0 +1,31 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-generate-booking-ids-deterministically-no-cancellation-yet
+type: decision-log
+title: "Generate booking ids deterministically, no cancellation yet"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`book(request)` must return a booking `id`. The determinism directive forbids randomness or wall-clock
+reliance in the service's logic. Separately, the request explicitly describes a cancellation policy
+(rule 5) but says not to implement cancellation yet.
+
+## Decision
+
+Generate ids as `booking-<n>`, where `n` is the 1-based count of bookings made so far on that service
+instance (`bookings.length + 1`), rather than a UUID or a timestamp-based id. This keeps booking creation
+fully deterministic and dependency-free. Cancellation itself (and any refund logic implementing rule 5)
+was left out of this change entirely: no `cancel` method, no stored cancellation state. The rule is only
+reflected as a comment-free scope boundary — nothing in the current API models it, since adding an unused
+hook for it now would be speculative.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Booking ids are stable and predictable for a given sequence of calls, which also makes tests
+  deterministic without needing to mock `Date.now` or a random source.
+- Ids are only unique within one service instance (the service is in-memory and per-process, so this
+  matches the current scope); a future persistence layer will need its own id strategy.
+- Implementing cancellation later will require adding a `cancel` operation and deciding how a cancelled
+  booking affects the overlap check in `book` (a cancelled booking's period should presumably free up the
+  slot); that design is deferred, not decided here.
